WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.310
VENKATESH RAMASWAMY:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.140
Welcome to a talk on switching
from DynamoDB to Cloud Bigtable

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.010
and Cloud Spanner.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.390
We had a lot of
learnings, and we

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:16.070
had a lot of fun
when we ended up

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.870
migrating from AWS
to Google Cloud,

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.670
and we'd like to share
our experiences with you.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.410
My name is Venkatesh Ramaswamy.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.960
I work for ShareChat
and my copresenter

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.500
is Vishal Parpia, who
works for Cloudcover.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.440
We've talked a little bit
about who we are and what we do

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.120
and fundamental
reasons as to why

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.370
you would choose
a migration, some

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.410
of the choice of
database and decisions

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.810
that we ended up going through.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.030
Some of the key design and
architectural principles

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.320
as well.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.020
Some of the details around
data migration, batch,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.450
and stream processing
will also be discussed.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.790
We will delve a little bit
on performance and audit

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.800
and why those are
important, and finally,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.570
end up with some
of the learnings

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.270
that we had through the process.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.400
A little bit on who
we are and what we do.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.430
Let me talk about ShareChat,
the company that I work for.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:12.820
We were founded in 2015, and
our latest series D funding

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.970
was led by Twitter
in August of 2019.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.450
We are India's home grown, and
one of the largest social media

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.520
platforms out there,
where we don't

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.970
offer English as an option.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.200
As of latest, we have about
150 million active users

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.700
on our website.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.850
We exist because there is
a fundamental disconnect

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.880
between the choice that
a lot of users in India

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.520
want versus what's
available out there.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.080
If you see the users in tier
2 and tier 3 series in India,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.050
a lot of them are not
comfortable in conversing

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.760
in English because that's
not their native language,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.810
so we give them the
language of their choice

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.270
that they could use
to end up interacting,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.010
conversing, and
sharing social media

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.140
posts with people around it.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.590
Very recently, we launched
Moj, a short video

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.320
app that we will spend a
little bit of limelight

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.710
towards the end of
this presentation.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.490
I'll now hand it over
to Vishal to talk

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.585
about him and his company.

00:02:11.585 --> 00:02:12.960
VISHAL PARPIA:
Thanks, Venkatesh.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:13.940
I'm Vishal.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.370
I'm the CEO and co-founder
for a Cloudcover.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.500
We are a premier partner
with Google Cloud,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.770
and we specialize in moving
companies like ShareChat

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.020
from one cloud to the other,
or from their data center

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.550
to the cloud.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:30.710
Our core focus is on agile
enterprises and scale startups,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.330
and we use a lot
of new technology,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.970
including cloud
native tech, in order

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.990
to make that happen with
DevOps and data engineering.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.090
So I'm going to
talk specifically

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.240
about what we did with
ShareChat, and at a very

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.910
high level, it always
looks really easy.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.430
On both AWS and Google
Cloud, you effectively

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.830
have an app, a service,
and a database.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:55.280
But as we all know, the
devil is in the details,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.200
and I'm going to hand
it back to Venkatesh

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.670
to give you a little
bit of insight

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.950
as to the unique
challenges that we faced

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.350
in this particular migration.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.850
VENKATESH RAMASWAMY:
Thanks, Vishal.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.460
As we are now
looking at what you

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.730
need to do when you move from
one cloud system to the other,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.710
you have to look at the
fundamental challenges

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.060
that you would encounter.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.610
In our case, we
were on ECS, and we

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.490
wanted to be completely
on GKE when we would

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.710
finish the migration process.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.710
CloudWatch was our choice for
all the logging and monitoring

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.186
on AWS.

00:03:26.186 --> 00:03:28.670
On Google, it will have
to be Stackdriver logging

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.680
and monitoring,
with the key note

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.460
that we would need 100% of
it to be available on day one

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.420
for all observability
requirements.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.740
On AWS, we were completely
on Elastic cache

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.780
using Redis as a storage engine.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.030
On Google Cloud, we
would want to end up

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.010
being on Managed Redis,
and even though they

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.350
were sort of almost
the same company,

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.960
it is not exactly
apples to apples,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.430
and that's something that
we had to contend with.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.170
Now, data migration
is a very key piece

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.830
of the entire process,
and we were sure

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.860
that we had to meet
100% of our data

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.250
even when a single user
had to be migrated over

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.390
from AWS to Google Cloud.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.920
Ultimately, we needed a
service mesh integration

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.830
to get better insights
into service dependencies,

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:20.100
and we had to do all of
this with zero downtime.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.560
When you start the migration
and get through the process,

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.710
it's like drinking
from a firehose.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.960
We hope to share with you
some of our key learnings,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.810
some of our key challenges,
through the next set of slides

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.600
to make sure that you are
not in the same situation,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.030
and you do a much better
job with all the learnings

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.760
and challenges that
we ended up with.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.710
So let's fundamentally
ask ourselves,

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.210
why should we migrate?

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.980
Let me walk you through some
of the thoughts that we had,

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.210
which started at around
middle of last year,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.250
where we were hitting
limitations of scale

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.750
with DynamoDB.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.072
Now, we partially solved
them through the use of DAX,

00:04:55.072 --> 00:04:56.530
but they were just
not good enough.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.140
We were still hitting a lot of
challenges during peak times,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.200
so when we ended up looking
deeper into what we had to do,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.620
we came to the conclusion
along with consultation

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.410
from AWS that we had to rewrite
a lot of our key and core

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.520
systems.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.640
Now, in that situation,
we wondered, was there

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.990
another option?

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.150
Was there something we
could do from the ground up?

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:18.620
And should we look at moving
to another cloud provider

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.160
when, ultimately, the set of
changes that we needed to do

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.420
were pretty dramatic
to begin with?

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:26.390
The other key factor was
that we had to save costs,

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:29.290
and this was extremely important
given the kind of scale

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.490
and the kind of
traffic that we had.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.540
We also were looking for a
very key and strategic partner

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.120
for our long term set of
challenges that we would

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.930
ultimately end up
in [INAUDIBLE],,

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.790
and we had very unique use cases
that we were encountering month

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.050
after month.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.670
Ultimately, we needed a
platform for innovation,

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.180
and that's why we
looked across the board,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.870
and Google Cloud came out
tops in our evaluation

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.980
as to the platform of choice.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.380
Let me give you a
little bit of the stats

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.590
and go over some of the key
stats on our existing system

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.000
using DynamoDB.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.180
We had a total of about
200 in all tables that we

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.370
had to migrate and about
80 terabytes of data.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.150
As an example, a single
table had close to what 17

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.650
secondary indexes--

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.870
obviously not extremely
optimized, but something

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.590
that we had to account for when
we end up doing the migration.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:24.960
At peak, our total read units
were at about five million QPS,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.140
and that was a lot of data that
we had to contain and make sure

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.790
that we don't take a hit.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.040
And ultimately, our
read and write agencies

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.380
were at the order of
about six milliseconds,

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.740
and that's a very
critical number

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.320
that we could not compromise on.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.320
Let's start with the choice
of databases, and ultimately,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.820
here's [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.220
For any migration,
you have to make sure

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.540
that the data considerations
are taken care of from day one.

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.880
You solve for the
data, and I can almost

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.610
guarantee that the bulk of your
problems and your pain points

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:00.110
will be automatically
taken care of.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:02.800
So there's one learning that you
want to take away, it probably

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.140
is that.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:08.210
Now, we had a polyglot
setup in our systems.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.800
We had a lot of DynamoDB, a
lot of Redis, Mongo, Cassandra,

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.125
Elastic, S3-- you
name it, we had it.

00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:16.000
And we also needed a
NoSQL that would support

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.000
secondary indexes, so we had
fundamentally four choices

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.160
that we could end
up choosing from.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.710
Choice number one, we could
go with a single cloud managed

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:24.670
database.

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.548
Life is easy, and you could just
make one choice and move on.

00:07:27.548 --> 00:07:29.840
Choice number two, we could
still make a single choice,

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.200
but this time, we would choose
a self managed database,

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.440
completely to be taken
care of from our end.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.600
Choice number three would
be a hybrid solution,

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.780
but optimized for cost, and
ultimately, the fourth choice

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.780
would be another hybrid
solution but optimized

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.710
for long-term [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.100
Looking at option
one and option two,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.100
it's a very clear choice between
choosing a single database

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.160
that's provided by cloud,
that's cloud native, which

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.430
is very easy to migrate,
but would be extremely

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.180
complex for some use
cases where you don't

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:00.820
need all that functionality.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.410
Or would you want to go self
managed, where you would take

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.280
on the burden of maintaining
all the different databases

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:07.670
[INAUDIBLE] experts?

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.670
But now, in my opinion, a
self managed database actually

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.350
nullifies the impact
and the benefits

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.683
of why you would actually
choose one in the first place.

00:08:14.683 --> 00:08:16.200
It's useful for a
lot of situations,

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.780
but not in our use case.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.960
If you look at option 3, one
of the things we could do

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.990
was put all our index and
secondary index needs,

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.120
we could choose and
store them in a database

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.500
that actually supported that.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.780
In this case, that
would be Cloud Spanner.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.150
And all the raw data, the
data on the leaf nodes,

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.520
notes, we could store
it in a database that

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.250
didn't require any special
indexing requirements, which

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.000
would be Cloud Bigtable.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.210
So the data in a
single table would

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.140
be split across two tables.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:48.310
This would make reads and
writes a little interesting,

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.430
and would require applications
or at least a layer

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.490
to handle those reads
and writes interestingly

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.860
on the app side of things
instead of the database

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.740
side of things.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.980
Let's look at option
4, which is ultimately

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.480
what we ended up choosing.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.630
This is getting the database
to do what it's really good at.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.960
So we ended up using
Cloud Bigtable for all

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.360
our single index
database requirements,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.520
table requirements and
storage requirements.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.210
And we used Cloud Spanner
for all the requirements

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.630
where you needed more
than a single index.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:22.310
So this allowed us to end up
using the database for what

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.250
it was built for, for
what it was really good at

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:29.300
and taking and moving the burden
from the application owners

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.310
to the databases and
leveraging them for what

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.310
they are really good at.

00:09:34.423 --> 00:09:36.840
To talk a little bit about the
key design and architecture

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.630
principles, for that, I want
to have Vishal come and spend

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:40.740
some time.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.670
VISHAL PARPIA:
Thanks, Venkatesh.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.300
So after we help ShareChat
out to figure out

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:49.020
which options worked best
for their specific use case,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.080
we had to then get
into the actual design

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.720
of how we would make these
work and how we would actually

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.250
enact the migration itself.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.620
The concept that made
sense for us over here,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.900
was to use a data
abstraction layer.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:06.710
Now a data abstraction layer is
a fairly common design pattern

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:08.300
in software engineering.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:13.310
And it also takes the guise of
something like a middleware.

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:16.610
In the case of ShareChat, they
were already using libraries

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:20.480
internally to kind of
obfuscate DynamoDB and provide

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.620
that functionality
to the application.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.490
So we wanted to take
advantage of that.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.990
The traditional approach would
have had the data abstraction

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.010
layer live inside of service.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.070
So literally inside the code.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.410
But because we were
moving to Kubernetes,

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.850
we had the opportunity of
using a sidecar approach

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.300
to make the same thing happen.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.010
And this gave us a few
really key benefits

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.530
that we would have otherwise
not been able to get.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.150
This modified sidecar
approach effectively

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:50.090
took the shape of
a database driver

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.940
that ran as a sidecar
process and allowed

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.390
us to do things like
connection pooling for the pod.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.700
We were also able to
do a single driver

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.950
for multiple different
kinds of languages

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.900
and just have a different
library that would

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.030
interact with it over GRPC.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:08.030
And this obviously gave us a
dramatic performance increase

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:10.340
in terms of the
kind of code that we

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.890
were running, because we
wrote everything in Go.

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.140
And it also allows
us to, in the future,

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.672
potentially change the
database redirections.

00:11:17.672 --> 00:11:19.880
And this pattern worked so
well, that we are actually

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.800
going to open source
this with Google as well.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.300
The critical piece
over here though,

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.360
was that ShareChat
didn't need to change

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:28.850
anything inside their
application layer at all.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.100
They just need to swap out
the library that was being

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:32.650
used to access the database.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:34.820
And this gave us
an enormous benefit

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.640
in terms of the speed with
which we could actually

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.180
make this migration occur.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.860
So I'm going to talk about
that migration in a little bit

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:44.220
more detail.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.550
This was obviously a
fairly gargantuan task.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.900
As you saw with the early slides
that Venkatesh showed you,

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.650
we were talking about
a substantial number

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:54.160
of both tables, as well as data.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.270
And in order for us to
be able to move this over

00:11:56.270 --> 00:12:00.260
from AWS to Google in a
repeatable and reliable manner,

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:03.300
we had to use a
multi-stage process.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.930
So the three phases that we
ended up with effectively

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:07.200
looked like this.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:12.740
We had a starting with a batch
of an upload of all of the data

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.540
until a specific point in time.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.100
And at the moment
that we actually

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.920
started that dump of
data from DynamoDB,

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:21.530
we also started inserting
all the mutations

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:25.580
that occurred to the
DynamoDB tables into Pub/Sub.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.400
We were going to use that
in T1, which was basically

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:30.170
after we were done
with the load,

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.780
we wanted to be able to play
back all the changes that

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.970
happened to the database
after we had loaded

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:37.230
that point in time snapshot.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:40.680
Following that, and this
is the most crucial phase,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.960
when we were actually
sort of in sync,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.930
we wanted to be able to
start streaming data directly

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:48.030
from AWS to Google.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.640
And this was probably one of
the most complicated parts

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.800
of the entire system,
because we couldn't

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.120
use Pub/Sub to do this.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.880
We had to build our
own system to enable

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.130
that kind of streaming
from DynamoDB

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:04.430
directly into both Cloud
Bigtable and Cloud Spanner.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:06.840
So let's talk about
the batch processing.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:08.990
So we took exports
of the table data

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:12.170
from DynamoDB, which
ended up inside S3.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.210
And we would basically
watch and consume those

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:15.860
and stuff them into
an SQS queue that

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.500
would be process by
EC2 instances that

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:22.910
would both compress and
transfer that over to GCS.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.460
On the Google side, we
were watching and consuming

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.920
those buckets through
Cloud Functions that

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:32.540
were triggering individual
DAGs inside of Airflow, which

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:35.300
was running on Cloud Composer,
that would run on top

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:39.230
of dataflow multiple
jobs to both load data

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:43.063
into Cloud Spanner, as well as
Cloud Bigtable simultaneously.

00:13:43.063 --> 00:13:44.480
And this, obviously,
was dependent

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.300
upon the configurations that
we had as to which table

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.030
went into which system
on the basis of where

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:51.290
the indexes were.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.840
So all of the tables that had
multiple indexes would end up

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:57.050
in Cloud Spanner, and all the
tables that had single indexes

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:00.556
would end up in Cloud Bigtable
and give us better performance.

00:14:00.556 --> 00:14:05.280
The load process itself was
also extremely complicated.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.220
As you can imagine,
firstly, we're

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.590
trying to take data that's
inside a NoSQL system

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:12.710
and put it inside a SQL system
in the case of Cloud Spanner.

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:15.690
That required us to
actually add missing columns

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:19.460
when we detected them inside
the data that we were getting.

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.640
Because a lot of
this was legacy data,

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:23.900
they weren't very
stable schemas,

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.030
and taking advantage
of NoSQL allows

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:29.930
you to effectively create keys
and values as you see fit.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.060
Well, that information
wasn't present

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.330
when we dumped the data in
some kind of format that

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:39.020
would allow us to create
the tables before we

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:39.930
did the transfer.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.140
So we had to actually
do that on the fly.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.930
So the code that we
wrote inside dataflow

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:48.800
would actually process all
of those individual rows

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:52.340
and add the appropriate tables,
column families, and missing

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:54.360
columns as required.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.790
We also had a dead
letter pattern

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.760
that dealt with rejected records
and allowed us to retry them

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.620
after we had made the
appropriate changes

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:05.660
to either the data type
or the schema itself.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:07.850
Once we were done with
that initial transfer,

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:11.030
we had to switch to doing
the stream processing.

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:13.770
This is where it started
getting really interesting.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:17.840
So in order to make this happen,
we used the system on DynamoDB

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.580
called DynamoDB Streams.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.140
Now what that does,
is it effectively

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:23.060
puts all the mutations
that are happening

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:27.290
inside DynamoDB into a
stream that can be consumed.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.150
And it retains the data
for about 24 hours.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.790
The lambda functions
that get triggered

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.010
on the back of that
had to be configured,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.698
because it got really
expensive really fast.

00:15:36.698 --> 00:15:38.240
So we set those up
so that they would

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:42.140
take batches of 10,000 records
in five minute intervals

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.320
and push those over
to some custom code

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.140
that we wrote on the
Google site running

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.590
on Compute Engine written in
golang that would allow us

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.380
to both push the
data in real time

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.570
into Cloud Spanner
and Cloud Bigtable,

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:57.950
depending upon
the configurations

00:15:57.950 --> 00:15:59.520
that we had in place.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:03.180
And this is really what
allowed us to do this.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:07.830
We tried to use Pub/Sub and
there were multiple concerns

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.750
that we ran into with that.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:12.200
Some of them were latency
related and some of them

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.535
were related to
ordering of the records

00:16:14.535 --> 00:16:16.910
in order to make sure that it
was going into the database

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:19.288
systems at the right time.

00:16:19.288 --> 00:16:21.080
As you can imagine,
latency and performance

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.000
is absolutely key over here.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.910
And we actually built a whole
bunch of interesting code

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.820
to be able to manage any
kind of breaks that might

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:32.710
occur in the stream itself.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:34.830
So after we were done
with that transfer,

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:38.100
it fell to our team to
make sure that Venkatesh

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.790
was happy with the outcome.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:42.060
And that meant making sure
that the data actually

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:43.620
matched on both systems.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.220
And that's where this comes in.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:46.410
Venkatesh.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:47.910
VENKATESH RAMASWAMY:
Thanks, Vishal.

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.450
Performance and audit
are two understated steps

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:52.800
in any migration process.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.460
They never get due diligence.

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:58.050
And I want to talk a little
bit about what we did to end up

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.430
focusing on these two aspects.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:02.367
So you always start
with data that's

00:17:02.367 --> 00:17:03.450
publicly available, right?

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.940
It's great to have data
which is a starting point,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.440
but you make sure
that you verify it.

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:13.020
So what we did was, to set up
a dummy setup in our staging

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:13.569
environment.

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:15.329
So in this example
that I have for you,

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.720
we had a 40 node cluster for
real table called tagPosts,

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:23.710
which was set up on Spanner,
which had about 160 GB of data

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:25.170
with about five indexes.

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:30.000
And we hammered it with a
readout ratio of a 4 to 1.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.050
This allowed us to see how
this Spanner table actually

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.600
scaled with the amount of
traffic that it was getting,

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.650
and what was its breaking point.

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:42.060
And once you had that, the next
key part and the most important

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:44.560
part, is to try the
same in production.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:46.920
And the reason for that
is, your physical setup

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:48.780
for staging and
production are going

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.150
to be fundamentally different.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:52.410
And you do this and
you rinse and repeat

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:54.960
for most of the key
flows and the key tables

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:57.600
that you would expect
to hit a lot of traffic

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.370
during normal and
during peak times.

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:02.310
And this would give
you a lot of comfort

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:06.170
when you turn on the migration
step completely onto one side.

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:11.660
The two different
audit parts that

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:15.710
are important for any
migration, are one for streaming

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:18.260
and the other, which we'll
talk about in the next slide.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:21.290
For streaming, it's extremely
important for us to make sure

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:25.250
that the data has been caught up
from the source and the system

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:27.710
output to the new system
that is under test.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:30.020
In this case, AWS
was the source output

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:32.520
and Google Cloud would
be the system under test.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.370
So the graph that we
have in front of you

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:36.830
was data that we
got from Influx,

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:38.600
which we were ending
up inserting into,

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:40.190
and visualized in Grafana.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:42.950
So you see that initially
at time t equal 0,

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.810
you have about 18
to 19 hours of lag.

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:48.210
And ultimately, it takes
about six hours for the reader

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:51.200
to actually catch up and
get close down to 0 as well

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:52.350
as possible.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:56.040
Again, you do this for your key
metrics, for your key tables.

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.980
And these are extremely
important to have

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:01.100
at your disposal to make sure
that your business metrics are

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:05.580
doing as well as
your system metrics.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.400
Now looking at audit
for data consistency,

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:10.800
the way we did this was
to make sure that we

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:14.310
replayed the events that were
happening on AWS on Google

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:17.490
Cloud, and did a simple JSON
diff of the two response

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:18.923
objects that we got back.

00:19:18.923 --> 00:19:20.340
And if there were
any differences,

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:21.780
we would end up logging that.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.408
So the values that you
see here are real values

00:19:24.408 --> 00:19:26.700
that we got when we initially
started out our migration

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:27.690
processes.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.910
And this allowed us
to make sure that we

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:31.390
fixed this, we
take care of this,

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:35.070
we handled this before we
shift even the first user over.

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:37.140
And that gave us
the confidence to go

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:39.660
extremely confidently
to business and say,

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:40.290
you know what?

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:42.840
The system is as good
as the business metrics,

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:46.380
and we should not see any
perceptible impact on users

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:50.483
as they end up moving from
one system to the other.

00:19:50.483 --> 00:19:52.900
And ultimately, I want to focus
a little bit on learnings.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.000
But before that, I
want to start off

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:58.990
with a small story which
started at around the first week

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:02.820
of July, not too long ago, when
we launched our short video

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.880
app called Moj, which took just
about 30 hours from ideation

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:08.500
to the actual launch.

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:09.170
And guess what.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:10.330
We were all ready.

00:20:10.330 --> 00:20:13.060
Google Cloud was ready
for us in terms of scale,

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:16.900
in terms of the kind of
traffic that we were expecting.

00:20:16.900 --> 00:20:19.930
Around the same time, because
of geopolitical reasons,

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:24.910
our traffic on the ShareChat
app also went astronomical.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.130
We went to almost
3x, 4x amount of data

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:29.260
that we were typically used to.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.030
And we didn't even blink an eye.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:34.480
We were able to horizontally
scale with zero to minimal code

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:36.220
changes.

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:38.080
Close enough for about
a week from then,

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:41.320
we made the decision to
move all of the traffic

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:44.440
for Moj, which was quite
a bit, on to Singapore.

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:48.040
And all this really happened
behind the scenes seamlessly,

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:50.440
thanks to the amazing
support from Google

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:51.810
that we ended up receiving.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:56.110
Care for minimal changes that
we had to do on the DNS entries,

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:59.080
we really didn't have much
in terms of challenges

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:01.480
that we faced during
that shift over.

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:04.360
And ultimately, it's
kudos to everybody

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:05.910
who has been involved in this.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:09.220
And I wanted to use this as the
poster child for the benefits

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:12.100
that we have gotten just three
months after the migration

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:14.230
process.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:16.230
And ultimately, the
learnings from the migration

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.450
itself, I think it's
important to know

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:21.630
that the migration or any
migration of this nature

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.550
is not going to
be a linear path.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.380
You could plan for
all you want, you

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.240
could build on the
contingencies in place,

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.730
you would still end
up encountering things

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:30.780
of the unknown nature.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.160
And you just have to
fight your way through it.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:34.650
But what's very
key and important

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.650
is the top down ownership
and the commitment

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:40.283
that you need to have to
make sure to get through all

00:21:40.283 --> 00:21:41.700
these challenges
that come you way

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:46.380
and sign up for long weekends
and nights that will ultimately

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:47.710
be the outcome.

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:49.680
Solve your DB choices first.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:50.920
Extremely important.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:52.070
I've mentioned this before.

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:53.610
I'm going to keep
mentioning this.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:57.210
Your data is going to be
the ultimate decider of how

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:58.540
good your migration process is.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:01.030
Your data doesn't
get moved correctly,

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:03.060
you can have a
number of challenges,

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:04.770
even with all the
computes in place, even

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.720
with all the scalable inputs.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.300
And then ultimately,
separate out your migration

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.590
and your optimization tasks.

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:12.510
A lot of the people
want to end up

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:14.857
doing one step of the
migration process,

00:22:14.857 --> 00:22:16.440
then ending up putting
an optimization

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.740
step on top of that.

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:19.180
It doesn't work very well.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:21.000
In fact, it's chaotic.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:23.520
It's extremely disastrous.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:25.140
We didn't do it
and we would never

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:27.590
do it, even if we had to
redo this all over again.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:28.770
There's a lot of learnings.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:31.050
But the key part is,
separate them out.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.030
Make sure that you
focus on the migration.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.330
If you have cost
concerns, talk to Google,

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:36.690
talk to your cloud provider.

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.320
I'm sure they'll have a
path forward for you guys.

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.820
And ultimately, work as
a single team, folks.

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:43.080
I can say this enough.

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:46.390
We had a lot of different
parties associated with this.

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:49.290
We had developers, we
had a Google PSO team,

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:52.620
we had actual Google engineers
from Spanner, from Bigtable,

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:54.360
from code engineering teams.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.820
We had our set of consultants.

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:59.790
We had CloudCover, who
was our partner in crime.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:01.710
And we worked as a
single unit, which

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:03.798
means that if I had
feedback to give,

00:23:03.798 --> 00:23:05.340
I would not just
give it to my folks,

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:07.390
I would give it to everybody.

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:11.430
So finally, I want to thank
all of these people involved.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:16.380
An amazing set of aligned
work, amazingly focused

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.360
set of people that were
involved in this process.

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:21.990
It would be impossible to end
up thanking everybody that

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:23.760
made this possible,
but I still want

